  `KeatingGTProvider` hardcodes `locale={gtConfig.defaultLocale}` (`"en-CA"`), unlike the web counterpart in `web/src/i18n/general-translation.tsx`, which detects the user's language via `getBrowserLocale()`/`supportedLocale()`. `gt-react-native`'s `GTProvider` resolves the device locale (via `getLocale()` → `getNativeLocales()`) only when `locale` is left unset, so explicitly pinning it means a French-Canadian device always renders English and the declared `"fr-CA"` locale can never activate — defeating the purpose of the new i18n wiring and the `<T>`/`<Num>`/`useGT` usage across the mobile screens. Impact is currently latent because `mobile/src/_gt/fr-CA.json` is empty `{}`, but it becomes user-visible once translations are generated in CI. Fix by omitting the pinned `locale` (letting GT resolve the device locale) or reading it via `expo-localization` and normalizing to a supported locale as the web helper does.
